electrophilic aromatic substitution can  be used to introduce a nitro group onto  an aromatic ring the Nitro group is an  interesting functional group because no  matter how we draw the lewis structure  we cannot avoid having formal charges on  it now if we just focus on the extra  electron pair that's making the formal  negative charge on this single bonded  oxygen we can actually represent this as  the negative charge and then see how the  movement of this electron pair to make a  new double bond here can push electrons  on to the other oxygen giving it the  formal negative charge we can show our D  localization a bit better by showing  this representation however this is not  convenient for showing arrow pushing in  the nitration reaction benzene can be  reacted with a mixture of sulfuric and  nitric acid to form nitro benzene the  mechanism begins with nitric and  sulfuric acid reacting to form the  nitronium ion in the first step the  stronger acid protonates  the weaker acid but wait a second nitric  and sulfuric acid are both strong acids  so how can we tell which is stronger  well we can look at a PKA table and see  that nitric acid has a pKa of minus 1.3  and sulfuric acid has a pKa of negative  3.0 but we can also look at stabilizing  effects of the conjugate bases remember  when the conjugate base of any acid is  more stabilized the compound is more  likely to give up a proton because it  has stabilization when that proton is  gone let's take a moment to look at the  resonance forms of nitric and sulfuric  acid and see if we can rationalize  sulfuric acid being the stronger acid we  can show the localization in the  conjugate base of nitric acid by pushing  electrons from the oxygen anion through  the double bonded oxygen this gives us a  total of three resonance forms for the  conjugate base of nitric acid now let's  look at the conjugate base of sulfuric  acid here we're just showing the first  proton coming off because that is the  one that ionizes completely  second one does not in each of these  forms we're going to maintain six bonds  to sulfur but we're going to push  electrons so that we have our oxy anion  forming sulfur oxygen double bonds we  can see for the conjugate base of  sulfuric acid we also have three  resonance forms so what's the difference  why is sulfuric acid a stronger acid in  the conjugate base of sulfuric acid we  actually have another effect going on  this inductive electron withdrawing  effect further stabilizes the negative  charge in the conjugate base of sulfuric  acid making this more stabilized and  thus a stronger acid returning to our  mechanism we can now show nitric acid  getting protonated by sulfuric acid if  we protonate the oxygen containing the  hydrogen atom this will make water which  is a good leaving group so the product  of this step is a protonated form of  nitric acid and the conjugate base of  sulfuric acid which we looked at in  detail below now we have two formal  positive charges next to each other in  this molecule so we want to relieve this  repulsive force and break off water  however if we just made water leave we  would end up with a two plus charge on  nitrogen which isn't very favorable at  all fortunately we have that oxy anion  right next door that can push down its  electrons assisting in the departure of  the leading group and leaving nitrogen  with just a plus one charge elimination  of the leading group gives the nitronium  ion and a molecule of water  now the nitronium ion is a powerful  electrophile and so benzene will open up  its double bond and attack it now we  have a carbo cation on benzene that  needs to be neutralized it can be  neutralized by the deprotonation of this  hydrogen atom now in this mechanism it  might be tempting to take the conjugate  base of sulfuric acid to do this  deprotonation however remember how  resonance stabilized that was from above  it's not going to be a very good  nucleophile because it's electrons are  tied up 